        en: 'A numerical sequence is a function defined on the positive integers: the argument is the index $n$, the value is the term $a_{n}$. We define a sequence either by an explicit formula $a_{n}=f(n)$, which gives any term immediately, or by a recursive formula, which supplies a starting term and a rule producing the next term from the previous one. Recursion is natural for modelling, but to reach $a_{100}$ you must pass through every earlier term — which is why it pays to be able to turn a recursion into an explicit formula. We test monotonicity by the sign of the difference $a_{n+1}-a_{n}$, and for a sequence with positive terms it is often easier to compare the ratio $\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}}$ with one. The decisive words are "for every $n$": checking the first few terms proves nothing, and with the ratio you must remember that for negative terms the inequality reverses. A sequence is bounded when all its terms lie between two constants; boundedness and monotonicity are independent, but together they guarantee convergence. An index is a natural number, so every equation in $n$ ends with rejecting the solutions that are not.',
