        en: 'A sequence is geometric when the ratio of two consecutive terms is constant: $\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}}=q$. This forces every term to be non-zero and $q\\neq 0$ — an assumption worth remembering, since it is what rejects spurious solutions in parameter problems. The $n$-th term is $a_{n}=a_{1}q^{\\,n-1}$, again with exponent $n-1$, and the sum of the first $n$ terms is $S_{n}=a_{1}\\cdot\\frac{1-q^{n}}{1-q}$ — useless for $q=1$, where simply $S_{n}=na_{1}$. The middle-term property becomes $a_{n}^{2}=a_{n-1}a_{n+1}$; note the square, not a mean, so taking a root gives $|a_{n}|$ and the sign must be settled separately. Monotonicity depends on the sign of $a_{1}$ and the position of $q$ relative to $1$, and for $q<0$ the sequence alternates and is not monotone at all. A negative ratio is entirely legitimate, and discarding it "because it looks odd" is a common error. In problems with two conditions the system usually reduces to a quadratic in $q$, which almost always has two solutions, both of which must be checked.',
